Colour blindness is X-linked recessive. The woman has normal vision but her father is colour blind, and a father passes his only X chromosome to every daughter, so she is a carrier: \(\text{X}\text{X}^{\text{c}}\). Her husband has normal vision: \(\text{X}\text{Y}\).

\(\text{X}\text{X}^{\text{c}} \times \text{X}\text{Y}\) gives four equally likely progeny:
- \(\text{X}\text{X}\): normal daughter;
- \(\text{X}^{\text{c}}\text{X}\): carrier daughter with normal vision;
- \(\text{X}\text{Y}\): normal son;
- \(\text{X}^{\text{c}}\text{Y}\): colour blind son.

One of the four, that is 25% of the progeny (half of the sons and none of the daughters), is colour blind. So the answer is option (A) 25%.

(a) Any two corresponding differences:

| Humoral immune response | Cell-mediated immune response |
