(i) The lac operon in the switched-on condition (inducer present) is drawn as a stretch of DNA with these labelled parts and events:
- the **regulator gene i**, with its own promoter, transcribed into **repressor mRNA**, which is translated into the **repressor** protein;
- the **promoter p** and the **operator o** of the operon, followed by the three structural genes **z, y and a**;
- the **inducer** (lactose, or allolactose) binding to the repressor, which converts it into an **inactive repressor** that cannot bind the operator;
- with the operator free, RNA polymerase transcribes the structural genes into one polycistronic **lac mRNA**;
- translation of the lac mRNA into the three enzymes: **\(\beta\)-galactosidase** (from z), **permease** (from y) and **transacetylase** (from a).

(ii) It is called negative regulation because the operon is switched off by a repressor: in the absence of the inducer, the repressor binds to the operator and prevents RNA polymerase from transcribing the operon, so the regulatory protein acts by inhibiting gene expression.
